WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.810
A derivative begins with an average rate between two different inputs

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:08.819
For the square function, choose three and three plus h

00:00:08.819 --> 00:00:14.833
The quotient measures vertical change divided by horizontal change, giving the secant line its slope.

00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:21.173
Expand the square to get nine plus six h plus h squared

00:00:21.173 --> 00:00:26.213
Subtract nine, factor out h, and cancel it while h remains nonzero

00:00:26.213 --> 00:00:30.833
Each algebra step exposes the nearby slope without dividing by zero.

00:00:32.133 --> 00:00:36.829
As the second input approaches three, the slope approaches six

00:00:36.829 --> 00:00:40.115
The limiting secant becomes the tangent line

00:00:40.115 --> 00:00:48.567
This instantaneous rate is defined by nearby changes, not by directly substituting zero into the original difference quotient.

00:00:49.867 --> 00:00:52.544
A derivative does not always exist

00:00:52.544 --> 00:01:00.131
At the corner of absolute x, left-hand slopes approach negative one while right-hand slopes approach positive one

00:01:00.131 --> 00:01:05.933
The function is continuous there, but no single tangent slope fits both approaches.
